  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "Mycena rosea, commonly known as the Rosy Bonnet, is a small delicate mushroom characterized by its pale pink to rosy-colored cap and thin, fragile structure. This species belongs to the Mycenaceae family, a group of small woodland fungi commonly found decomposing plant material in moist forest environments.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Cap Color",
      "description": "Distinctive pale pink to rose-colored cap, typically 0.5-1.5 cm in diameter, which fades as the mushroom ages",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Gill Attachment",
      "description": "Gills are narrowly attached to the stem and are white to pale pink, with a serrated or toothed edge",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Delicate Structure",
      "description": "Very thin, fragile, translucent stem (stipe) that is typically white and may show slight pink hues near the base",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Rosy Bonnet thrives in moist deciduous and mixed forests of New England, growing on decaying leaf litter, wood chips, and decomposing organic matter on the forest floor.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "Like many Mycena species, the Rosy Bonnet exhibits bioluminescence, with some specimens capable of producing a faint glow in complete darkness—a phenomenon still not fully understood by mycologists.",
  "confusionNotes": null,
